Let me check a couple of claims — particularly Option D's concession about basil's multiple claim types and Option C's structural claim about document heterogeneity.
Good. Let me verify Option C's structural claim about basil vs the six shorter documents and check for basil's dual air-temp claim (optimum + injury threshold) which is critical to Option D's concession.
Good — confirms Option C's structural observation: six docs are ~200 lines, peppermint is ~450, basil is ~815. Let me now check basil for both optimum and injury/threshold data on air temperature, which is Option D's admitted weakness.
The file may have encoding or character issues with grep. Let me try viewing the temperature section directly.
This is the critical evidence. Lines 88-92 confirm that basil's air temperature section carries BOTH a growth optimum (25-30°C, line 88/103) AND injury data (12°C chilling injury, line 91/121-125) AND an experimental treatment (35°C supra-optimal, line 92/107-119). That directly validates the critique point about Option D: one row per crop × axis cannot hold these distinct claim types. Now I have what I need to produce the critique.

{"critic_id":"critic-simplicity","critiques":[{"target_option":"Option A","weakest_assumption":"That the heterogeneity of the corpus requires the complexity of composite foreign keys, role enums, and constrained interval child tables — when the payload is ~5 usable numbers and 674 structured absences.","likely_failure_mode":"The schema ships before seed data is curated, the team writes workarounds, and the safety constraints protect an empty table. Row-heavy joins over 64 logical cells of which 60 are NULL is a textbook case of building for imaginary future consumers.","hidden_cost":"Every future contributor must learn the role/basis/scope vocabulary before inserting or querying a single fact, and SQLite's lack of row-level permissions means the safety views are advisory anyway — one raw query bypasses the entire design.","best_fix":"Implement only the claim-role enum and the measurement-basis non-joinability constraint as CHECK constraints on a single flat table; defer the child interval table and composite FKs until a second crop batch actually produces conflicting join paths.","verdict":"reject"},{"target_option":"Option B","weakest_assumption":"That JSON1 generated columns and LIKE-based scorability detection are 'good enough' static checking — they are not; a misspelled key silently produces NULL and a status tag substring match is a bug waiting to happen.","likely_failure_mode":"A payload typo or a new status value like 'no_direct_evidence_yet' false-matches the scorability filter, and nobody notices because only 3-5 cells are supposed to be scorable so the difference between 3 and 4 is invisible.","hidden_cost":"Markdown-as-truth plus SQLite-as-cache sounds cheap, but keeping two representations in sync is its own maintenance burden, and every consumer must understand the JSON shapes — the complexity is not removed, just relocated from the schema to the application.","best_fix":"Replace LIKE-based scorability with an explicit boolean or enum column set during extraction, not derived from payload content; add a JSON schema comment or validation script that runs on INSERT.","verdict":"revise"},{"target_option":"Option C","weakest_assumption":"That deterministic parsing is safe for the six ~200-line documents — it checked the heading structure but not the value formats, and even for the six, the registry's 10-status vocabulary already requires semantic judgment that a deterministic parser cannot make.","likely_failure_mode":"Human review bottleneck stalls the pipeline for the 815-line basil document, the hash gate gets bypassed 'just once' during a demo, and the provenance guarantee — the entire point — is silently voided.","hidden_cost":"Maintaining three synchronized representations (markdown, JSONL intermediate, SQLite release) is triple the surface area for drift; the 45-minute validation spike assumes a curator who can read Korean technical prose, which is itself a scarce resource.","best_fix":"Drop the JSONL intermediate; curate directly into the flat SQLite table with a source_span column and a git-commit column, which gives you the audit trail without the triple-representation overhead.","verdict":"reject"},{"target_option":"Option D","weakest_assumption":"Its own author concedes it: one row per crop × axis cannot hold basil's air-temp optimum (25-30°C) AND its chilling injury threshold (12°C) AND its supra-optimal experimental point (35°C) — I verified all three exist in basil section 2. The steelman ('it is cheap') is true but the concession is not a corner case, it is the richest cell in the entire matrix.","likely_failure_mode":"The team adds a second row for basil air-temp injury data, then a third for the experimental treatment, then discovers they need role labels, and arrives at a worse version of Option B through accretion rather than design.","hidden_cost":"Hand-curation with no reproducibility means the next document revision silently desyncs the DB, and nobody has a script to detect it. The JSON blob is also not queryable, so the 674 absences — the corpus's actual payload — are opaque to SQL.","best_fix":"Keep the flat table but allow multiple rows per crop × axis with a mandatory claim_role column (optimum / injury / experimental / absence). This costs ~20 minutes of migration and fixes the fatal concession without the weight of Options A or C.","verdict":"revise"},{"target_option":"Option E","weakest_assumption":"That a per-context disposition engine (SCORABLE / ADVISORY_ONLY / RECORD_ONLY) will be exercised by more than one deployment context during this product's lifetime — the corpus supports 3-5 scored cells regardless of context, and canopy-RH-for-disease-risk scoring requires a labelled disease-risk use case that no brief has specified.","likely_failure_mode":"The disposition query is built, tested against the validation fixture's 8-element vector, and then called exactly once with exactly one context for the life of the product — the context-sensitivity machinery is dead code.","hidden_cost":"Every new deployment must populate its own calibration and context records before any channel lights up, which means the first-run experience is a blank dashboard until an expert configures it. That is worse UX than a hardcoded flag.","best_fix":"Hardcode the 3-5 scorable cells as a seed-data enum now; add the context-dispatch layer only when a second deployment context actually exists and asks for different eligibility.","verdict":"reject"},{"target_option":"Option F","weakest_assumption":"That three parallel tiers (scored band, coverage_status enum, section-9 prose) will make a dashboard with 2 numeric cards out of 8 feel useful rather than empty — this is a UX hypothesis, not an engineering guarantee, and the card's author pre-committed that ≤3 scored cells means 'abandon numeric scoring entirely', which the moderator has now confirmed.","likely_failure_mode":"The advisory and guidance tiers become the de facto product, but they are rendered as second-class decorations around the gauge-first layout that was never redesigned — users see mostly grey cards with small-print Korean prose they cannot act on.","hidden_cost":"Section-9 prose adaptation from Korean to actionable guidance in the UI was never budgeted, and the coverage_status enum is hand-curated, so the conflation risk does not disappear — it migrates from the number to the status label.","best_fix":"Accept the author's own pre-commitment: at 3 scored cells, redesign guidance-first (not gauge-first). Use F's three-tier data model but invert the UI hierarchy: guidance card is primary, scored band is a small inline indicator where it exists.","verdict":"revise"},{"target_option":"Option G","weakest_assumption":"That immutable revision tracking and two-file telemetry/knowledge separation will ever be queried or audited during a hackathon product — the knowledge base is ~200 facts that change at corpus-revision speed (months), not at telemetry speed.","likely_failure_mode":"The revision table accumulates exactly one revision, the ATTACH-based two-connection pattern introduces a bug in the first week, and nobody ever replays a historical dashboard state because the product has no regulatory or compliance requirement to do so.","hidden_cost":"Reversibility is rated 'hard' by the card itself — this is speculative infrastructure whose removal is more expensive than its construction, the exact opposite of a hackathon bet.","best_fix":"Ship one SQLite file. Add a schema_version integer and a loaded_at timestamp to the fact table header. If a compliance or audit requirement materialises, bolt on revision tracking then — it is an additive change, not a refactor.","verdict":"reject"},{"target_option":"Option H","weakest_assumption":"That the corpus is the only legitimate evidence base — the card itself notes that well-known general horticultural RH ranges (e.g. 60-80%) were deliberately excluded and whether that exclusion was correct is 'unassessed'. Steelmanning: the corpus was written precisely to prevent fabrication, so importing outside ranges would undermine its central discipline. Counter: a product that refuses to score 7 of 8 channels may provoke stakeholders into overriding with invented thresholds, which is worse than incorporating vetted general references with explicit provenance.","likely_failure_mode":"Stakeholders reject the reframing, override with unvetted thresholds, and the corpus's careful absence markers become irrelevant — the very outcome H exists to prevent.","hidden_cost":"Near-zero engineering cost, but high political cost: 'condition monitor' is a product demotion that must be sold to whoever wrote the brief, and H offers no strategy for that conversation.","best_fix":"Adopt H's honesty as the data layer's constraint but pair it with F's guidance-first UI (revised per my F critique) so the product has a positive value proposition — 'we show you what we know and what to do about what we don't' — rather than reading as 'we can't do what you asked'.","verdict":"revise"}],"overall_ranking":["Option D","Option H","Option B","Option F","Option E","Option C","Option A","Option G"],"decision_crux":"The sparsity argument and the safety argument are answering different questions but only the sparsity argument is load-bearing right now: with 3-5 scorable cells, the schema's job is storing 674 justified absences and qualitative guidance, not protecting a handful of numbers from conflation. That reframing rescues F and H but exposes A, C, E, and G as solving problems this corpus does not yet have. The cheapest viable path is revised-D (flat table, multiple rows per crop × axis with a claim_role column) paired with revised-H (condition monitor framing) and revised-F (guidance-first UI with inline scoring where evidence exists). Everything else is speculative infrastructure whose build cost is not justified by any query a hackathon product will run."}
